
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Database Relationships</title>
<style>
*{box-sizing:border-box}
body{font-family:Arial,sans-serif;background:#f4f0ff;color:#302650;margin:0;padding:25px}
header{text-align:center;background:#6c55bd;color:white;padding:28px;border-radius:18px}
main{max-width:850px;margin:25px auto}
.card{background:white;padding:22px;margin:16px 0;border-radius:14px;box-shadow:0 4px 12px #e1daf5}
.diagram{padding:15px;text-align:center;background:#eee8ff;border-radius:10px;font-weight:bold;line-height:2}
h2{color:#6c55bd}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<header><h1>🗄️ Database Relationships</h1><p>How database tables connect to each other.</p></header>
<main>
<section class="card">
<h2>One-to-One (1:1)</h2>
<div class="diagram">Person ───── Passport</div>
<p>One person has one passport record in this simplified example, and each passport record belongs to one person.</p>
</section>
<section class="card">
<h2>One-to-Many (1:M)</h2>
<div class="diagram">Teacher ───── Multiple Classes</div>
<p>One teacher can be assigned to several classes.</p>
</section>
<section class="card">
<h2>Many-to-Many (M:N)</h2>
<div class="diagram">Students ⇄ Courses</div>
<p>Many students can join many courses. A junction table, such as StudentCourse, can represent this relationship.</p>
</section>
<section class="card">
<h2>Why Are Relationships Important?</h2>
<ul>
<li>Connect related information.</li>
<li>Reduce unnecessary duplication.</li>
<li>Support accurate queries.</li>
<li>Help maintain data integrity.</li>
</ul>
</section>
</main>
</body>
</html>
